add('lesson:'+slug,'Container for distinct DNS roles, transport versus signed-data protections, trusted time and restricted management.',[
 'Profile replaces authored header. DNSSEC/transport section1 and time/NTS section2 remain optional Beyond the textbook; observed4.5/4.4 union is not exact child scope.',
 'These controls have different boundaries and failure modes. Reading the container or its subunits does not prove secure deployment, complete threat coverage or independent assessment.'
]);
add('keywords:'+slug,'Three short entries introduce policy-based DNS filtering, clock synchronization and network-management protocol purpose.',[
 'Known-harmful filtering does not guarantee accurate classification or catch every malicious connection. Plain NTP synchronization does not inherit cryptographic NTS protection.',
 'SNMP purpose is not secure configuration or write authorization; guide definitions are vocabulary rather than protocol operation or full hardening.'
]);
add('check:'+slug,'Reveal-only comparison explains why signed-answer validation and encrypted client-resolver transport are distinct.',[
 'DNSSEC works for authenticated signed data and trust-chain validation. DoT/DoH protects the selected transport under proper TLS endpoint authentication, not secrecy from that resolver or every subsequent connection.',
 'The authored model response is inspected; the reveal has no response input, automated key comparison, score or wrong-response teaching. No independent graded family.'
]);
add('section:'+slug+':0','Separate authoritative publication, recursive resolution and protective policy roles with access, administration and resilience context.',[
 'Recursive behavior can use cached answers/forwarders rather than walking every referral for every query. Transfer restrictions require appropriate authentication and authorization, not mere reliance on a port or address.',
 'Protective DNS is defense in depth: direct IP, alternate resolvers, changing infrastructure and false positives limit it. Resolution or blocking does not prove a connection, malicious intent, safe destination or complete visibility.'
]);
add('section:'+slug+':1','Optional comparison distinguishes DNSSEC origin/integrity validation, encrypted DNS transport and protective DNS policy.',[
 'DNSSEC neither encrypts queries nor makes signed content benign. Validate trusted anchors/chain and applicable signed data; transport encryption is not a substitute.',
 'DoT/DoH assumes properly authenticated/configured endpoints. Resolver sees the query; traffic analysis and endpoint compromise remain. DNS-based inspection may be bypassed by unmanaged encrypted resolver choice.',
 'Protective DNS does not establish every connection or maliciousness. Optional source teaching is not full standards conformance or approved mandatory exam scope.'
]);
add('section:'+slug+':2','Optional time-security context separates credible synchronization from authenticated exchange and operational monitoring.',[
 'NTS protects client-server NTP exchanges, not a compromised endpoint, dishonest trusted source or all delay/availability attacks. Cryptography alone cannot remove asymmetric packet-delay error.',
 'Approved sources, monitored paths/peers and drift alerts are operational choices, not proved accurate clocks or blanket peer guarantees. No actual log integrity, certificate-validity or authentication-window test is performed.'
]);
add('section:'+slug+':3','Management-plane teaching distinguishes SNMP query/configuration/events from suitable USM authentication/privacy/access settings.',[
 'SNMPv3 supports different security levels; the version label alone does not select auth/privacy. Appropriate privacy depends on needs; this is not a universal claim that every valid SNMPv3 exchange must encrypt.',
 'Legacy RFC3414 mechanism examples are not current approved algorithms; configure supported strong choices. Strong encryption does not remove excess write privileges, compromised credentials or unmanaged interfaces.',
 'Traps and informs differ in acknowledgment; source purpose vocabulary does not supply complete message/reliability or operational evidence. No management traffic is captured or configured.'
]);
add('traps:'+slug,'Three cautions distinguish signed DNS authenticity, conditional DoH privacy/policy bypass and configured SNMPv3 security.',[
 'Query privacy concerns the protected client-resolver channel, not invisibility to resolver/endpoints or all traffic analysis. DNSSEC does not certify destination safety.',
 'Read SNMPv3 caution with the source suitable-security-level context: authentication, authorization, credential management and privacy where required. No version-only secure-state inference.'
]);
